# Project rules for AI coding agents

This file is read automatically by Claude Code (`CLAUDE.md`) at the start of every session
in this repo. If you also use Codex, copy or symlink this same content to `AGENTS.md` —
Codex reads that file the way Claude Code reads this one. Keep the two in sync; a rule that
exists in only one place will silently not apply when the other agent is driving.

Everything below is a starting point pulled from a real production repo, then stripped of
anything project-specific. Delete what doesn't apply, keep what does, and add your own
house rules under a new heading as you discover them — the value of this file grows every
time an agent does something surprising and you write down the rule that would have
prevented it.

---

## Git & PR workflow

**First, confirm your repo's actual default branch.** Plenty of repos still use `master`,
`trunk`, or something else instead of `main` — run `git remote show origin | grep 'HEAD
branch'` (or check your Git host's settings) and use the real name everywhere below.
`main` in these examples is a placeholder, not an assumption about your repo.

**Check for uncommitted local changes before creating a branch, not just before destroying
one.** `git status`. `git checkout -b` carries any uncommitted changes in the working tree
onto the new branch — if those belong to something else (a parallel session's in-progress
edit, work you forgot was open), they ride along into your new branch and its eventual
commit. Commit, stash, or explicitly account for anything `git status` shows before you
branch, not only before a destructive command.

**Always branch from the remote default branch, never from whatever is currently checked
out.**

```
git fetch origin main && git checkout -b <your-branch> origin/main
```

(swap `main` for your repo's real default branch, confirmed above)

`git checkout -b <name>` with no start point branches from the current `HEAD`. If you (or a
parallel agent session, or a past version of yourself from an hour ago) left another
branch checked out, your new branch silently inherits all of its unmerged commits as
ancestors. If the repo squash-merges, that other work rides along inside your PR:
unreviewed, never tested on its own, and credited to a commit message describing something
else entirely.

Before pushing, verify with `git log --oneline origin/main..HEAD` (same substitution) — it
should list only your own commits. If it lists more, you branched from the wrong place;
stop and fix the base before pushing.

**Never commit directly to the default branch.** This matters even more if more than one
agent or person might touch this working directory — confirm the current branch
(`git branch --show-current`) before committing rather than assuming.

**"Merge when ready" means merge after CI reports green, not immediately on PR creation.**
Before merging, check that every required status check has actually passed — don't guess
from the PR being open a while, and don't assume a failure is something transient like
quota without reading the actual failure log.

**If you're running multiple agents in parallel against the same repo** (e.g. Claude Code
and Codex both making changes at the same time), give each one its own worktree or clone —
not just its own branch. A single working directory can only have one branch checked out
at a time; two concurrent processes sharing that directory will still read and write the
same files no matter which branch each one thinks it's on, and can overwrite or interleave
each other's edits. `git worktree add ../agent-2 -b <branch> origin/main` gets you an
isolated working directory on its own branch without a second full clone. When
consolidating their work back together, cherry-pick specific commits onto a fresh branch
from the remote default rather than `git merge`-ing one agent's branch into another's —
merging drags in that branch's full history and can trip pre-push hooks that expect a
linear, reviewed set of commits.

If you discover a branch, stash, or set of uncommitted changes you don't recognize,
investigate before deleting or overwriting it — it may be another session's in-progress
work. Prefer a reversible move (stash, rename, set aside) over a destructive one.

## Status: check, don't read

Status prose (README badges, a "done" section in a doc, a comment claiming something is
implemented) drifts out of sync with reality constantly, because nobody remembers to update
it every time the code changes. Treat it as a hint, never as a source of truth. For
anything shippable, `git log` and the actual code are authoritative — a doc that disagrees
with them is the thing that's wrong, not the code.

If your project tracks a backlog or status file, prefer one where claims are falsifiable:
a "done" entry that cites a specific commit or PR, ideally checked by a script in CI,
beats a checkbox someone ticked by hand and never revisited.

## Safety and file operations

Never delete or overwrite a file, folder, or branch without first confirming the exact
target path or ref with the user — "clean this up" is not the same as "delete
`/exact/path`". If your project has data that must never be touched by an agent (a media
folder, a user database dump, anything genuinely irreplaceable), say so explicitly here,
by name, with no exceptions — don't rely on the agent inferring it's off-limits.

Before any command that could discard uncommitted work (`git checkout --`, `git restore`,
`git reset --hard`, `git clean`, `rm -rf` inside the repo), run `git status` first and stash
or commit anything that's there.

## House style, by example

Codify small, repeatable conventions here so an agent doesn't have to be told twice. One
real example, so you can see the pattern: a team that decided user-facing copy should never
contain an em dash wrote it as a standing rule (`Em dashes are radioactive in user-facing
copy — never use one in a string a reader sees. This does not apply to code comments,
which may still use them freely.`) instead of correcting it in every PR review. Whatever
your own repeated corrections are — naming conventions, a banned pattern, a required check
before a certain kind of change — write them here the same way, as a rule with a reason,
not just a reminder.

## When one spot keeps needing fixes, stop and diagnose before patch three

If you've edited the same function or file more than twice for what looks like a new bug
each time, stop before writing the next patch and ask: is this actually several unrelated
bugs, or one fragile design producing a new symptom every round? A patch verified only
against the one scenario that was just reported doesn't test the *shape* of the problem —
it silently leaves the design just as capable of producing the next symptom, so the fix
count climbs while the underlying bug count doesn't actually drop. Each individual patch
can be completely correct and the loop still never converges, because "correct for the
reported case" and "correct in general" are different claims.

This exact loop happened building the review-watcher script in this pack
(`scripts/watch-codex-review.sh`): getting "poll for a bot's response within a timeout,
without polling forever or missing the answer" right took roughly twenty rounds of
automated review before anyone stopped to name what was actually happening. Most of those
rounds were narrow, verified, genuinely-correct fixes to the *one case just reported* —
and nearly all of them were the same underlying defect wearing a new trigger: a function
that discarded already-successful partial work the moment any one step inside it failed,
whether that step failed from a budget check, a timeout, or a real error. Patch after patch
closed the specific trigger without ever asking "what's the general version of this
failure?" The loop only ended once someone stepped back and asked that question directly,
then made failure non-fatal to already-completed work as a structural rule, instead of
special-casing the next way a step could fail.

Two things to actually do differently once you notice you're in this loop:

- **Name the general shape before writing the next patch.** If patch two and patch three
  are both edits to the same dozen lines for "another edge case," write down — in a
  sentence, doesn't need to be formal — what class of failure they're both instances of.
  Often the honest answer is "this can't be made narrowly correct, it needs restructuring,"
  and that's worth knowing before patch four.
- **If verifying each patch costs real external time** (a slow bot review, a live API call,
  a human in the loop) and you've paid that cost more than twice on the same problem, stop
  paying it per-iteration. Build a fast local repro — a mock, a stub, a fake input that
  triggers the same code path — so the next several attempts cost seconds instead of
  minutes each. Still paying the external round-trip tax for iteration five is itself a
  sign you're testing symptoms, not the actual fix.

## Multi-agent collaboration

If you use more than one AI coding tool against this repo (Claude Code, Codex, others),
decide and write down who's better suited to what, rather than re-deciding it every time:

- One agent as the primary driver for anything needing full project context or multi-file
  changes.
- A second agent (if it has capabilities the first doesn't — e.g. image generation, or a
  genuinely independent perspective for review) for self-contained tasks and adversarial
  second-opinion review.

**A second, independent reviewer on every PR that touches real application code is worth
defaulting to, not reserving for changes that look risky at a glance.** A routine-looking
PR is exactly the kind that slips through unreviewed — plenty of real projects have shipped
a PR with zero review that turned out to carry several correctness bugs, caught only in a
retrospective sweep well after release. Skip the extra review only for changes that can't
ship a user-facing bug (docs, tooling config, content edits).

When kicking off a task on a second agent, give it purpose and hard constraints, let it
propose its own plan, review the plan before it touches code, then review the diff after —
and still verify its claims independently before merging, the same as you would for a
change you wrote yourself. `scripts/watch-codex-review.sh <owner/repo> <pr-number>
--trigger` (in this pack) requests that review and polls for the response instead of you
checking back on the PR by hand.
